.TH PACKMULE 1 "2026-07-26" "packmule 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.\"
.\" packmule.1 \(em manual page for packmule(1)
.\"
.SH NAME
packmule \- resolve, download, and bundle packages for air-gapped environments
.\"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B packmule
.B \-f
.I manifest
.RB [ \-o
.IR output_dir ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR arch ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR os ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR ver ]
.RB [ \-u
.IR url ]
.RB [ \-j
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.br
.B packmule verify
.I bundle_dir
.br
.B packmule
.BR \-V | \-\-version
.br
.B packmule
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.\"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B packmule
reads a package manifest file, queries the appropriate registry to resolve
each package to a concrete version and download URL, downloads every required
file, and verifies each file against its published SHA\-256 or SHA\-512 digest.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-bundle ,
.B packmule
also writes a
.I manifest.json
index, a
.I SHA256SUMS
file, and a ready-to-run
.I install.sh
script into the output directory, then compresses the entire directory to a
single
.I .tar.gz
archive.
The archive can then be transported to a network-isolated machine and
installed without any outbound internet access.
.PP
Dependency resolution runs to a fixed point rather than in a single pass:
when a requirement discovered later narrows what an already-resolved package
must satisfy, that package is resolved again.
The result therefore does not depend on the order entries appear in the
manifest, and a requirement that cannot be satisfied fails the run before
anything is downloaded.
.PP
Packages that fail integrity verification are removed from the output
directory and reported on standard error.
The exit status reflects whether all packages were obtained successfully.
.\"
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI verify " bundle_dir"
Re-check an extracted bundle against its
.I SHA256SUMS
file and exit non-zero if any file is missing or does not match.
This is the same check
.I install.sh
performs before installing anything; it verifies that the bundle survived the
trip to the target intact.
.\"
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-manifest " manifest"
Path to the input manifest file.
This option is \fBrequired\fP.
The expected format depends on the registry backend selected with
.BR \-t :
a
.I requirements.txt
for the
.B pypi
backend, a
.I package.json
for
.BR npm ,
and a plain package-per-line text file for
.BR rpm .
.TP
.BI \-o " output_dir"
Directory in which to write the downloaded package files.
The directory is created if it does not exist.
Defaults to the current working directory
.RB ( . ).
Ignored when
.B \-n
is given.
.TP
.BR \-t " type" ", " \-\-type " type"
Select the registry backend.
When omitted, the backend is auto-detected from the manifest filename
.RB ( requirements*.txt
\(rA
.BR pypi ,
.B package.json
\(rA
.BR npm ,
.B packages.txt
\(rA
.BR rpm ),
falling back to
.B pypi
for unrecognised names.
Run
.B packmule \-\-help
for the full list of available types.
.TP
.BR \-a " arch" ", " \-\-arch " arch"
Target CPU architecture for platform-specific package selection.
Defaults to the current machine architecture as reported by
.BR uname (2)
(e.g.\&
.IR x86_64 ,
.IR aarch64 ,
.IR arm64 ).
When set, an architecture-specific package is preferred over a universal one;
both are preferred over a source archive.
Specify
.B any
to disable architecture preference and download only universal or source
packages.
The values
.B aarch64
and
.B arm64
are treated as equivalent (same CPU, different OS naming conventions).
.TP
.BR \-s " os" ", " \-\-os " os"
Target operating system for wheel selection
.RB ( pypi
backend only): one of
.BR linux ,
.BR macos ,
.BR windows ,
or
.B any
to disable OS filtering.
Defaults to the OS of the current machine.
.TP
.BR \-p " ver" ", " \-\-python " ver"
Target CPython version for wheel selection and environment-marker
evaluation
.RB ( pypi
backend only), e.g.\&
.IR 3.12 .
Accepts
.IR 3.12 ,
.IR 3.12.2 ,
.IR 312 ,
or
.IR cp312 .
Defaults to the version of the local
.B python3
interpreter; when none is found, wheels are matched by architecture only.
.TP
.BR \-u " url" ", " \-\-repo\-url " url"
Base URL of the upstream package repository.
Required when using the
.B rpm
backend.
Optional for
.B pypi
and
.B npm
\(em when omitted those backends use their default public endpoints
(pypi.org and registry.npmjs.org respectively).
Use this flag to point any backend at a private mirror or corporate proxy
such as JFrog Artifactory or Sonatype Nexus.
.RS
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.I https://dl.fedoraproject.org/pub/fedora/linux/releases/40/Everything/x86_64/os
.IP
.I https://artifactory.example.com/artifactory/api/pypi/pypi-virtual/pypi
.IP
.I https://nexus.example.com/repository/npm-proxy
.RE
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bundle
After all packages are downloaded successfully, write a
.IR manifest.json ,
a
.IR SHA256SUMS ,
and an
.I install.sh
into the output directory and then compress the entire directory to
.IB output_dir .tar.gz\c
\&.
.RS
.PP
The manifest is a JSON file listing each downloaded package with its resolved
version, filename, the digest published upstream, and the SHA\-256 of the
file as bundled.
.I SHA256SUMS
carries the same SHA\-256 digests in
.BR sha256sum (1)
format, covering the package files and the generated metadata;
.I install.sh
verifies it before installing anything.
.PP
The install script is pre-configured for the active registry backend:
.B pip install \-\-no\-index \-\-find\-links
for
.BR pypi ;
for
.BR npm ,
either
.B npm ci \-\-offline
replaying a bundled
.I package-lock.json
(lockfile bundles) or a single offline
.B npm install
over every bundled
.I .tgz
(flat bundles); and
.B dnf install \-\-disablerepo=\'*\'
(falling back to
.BR "rpm \-Uvh" )
for
.BR rpm .
.PP
Bundling is skipped in dry-run mode and skipped if any package failed to
download.
The output directory is left intact alongside the archive.
.PP
After bundling, packmule checks that the result actually installs offline: for
.B pypi
by running pip's resolver against only the bundled files, and for
.B npm
by running the generated
.I install.sh
against an unroutable registry with an empty cache.
A bundle the package manager rejects is a build failure; see
.BR \-\-no\-verify .
.RE
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Resolve every package in the manifest and print what would be downloaded,
but do not write any files to disk.
The registry is still queried, so the output reflects real resolved
versions, download URLs, and digests.
Useful for auditing a manifest before committing to a download.
.TP
.BR \-j " n" ", " \-\-jobs " n"
Number of downloads to run concurrently, from 1 to 16 (default 4).
Registry CDNs are latency-bound for the small files that dominate a
dependency closure, so overlapping transfers helps more than a faster single
stream.
.B \-j 1
downloads serially and restores the per-file progress bar.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip the post-bundle offline install check.
The check runs the real package manager against the bundle, so it needs that
package manager present and a target platform matching this machine; use this
flag to build a bundle without it.
.TP
.BI \-\-rpm\-deps " mode"
.RB ( rpm
backend only) whether to follow transitive dependencies:
.B resolve
(the default) walks
.I <rpm:requires>
in the repository metadata and bundles a provider for each capability, while
.B none
bundles only the packages named in the manifest.
.RS
.PP
.B resolve
bundles the full closure within the repository, which includes base-system
packages the target probably already has.
That is deliberate:
.BR dnf (8)
skips what is already installed, and a redundant package costs only disk
whereas a missing one cannot be fixed on an air-gapped machine.
Capabilities nothing in the repository provides are reported as warnings.
Boolean and rich dependency expressions are reported but not evaluated.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version banner and exit with status 0.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a brief usage summary to standard error and exit with status 0.
.\"
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All packages were resolved and downloaded successfully.
.TP
.B 1
One or more packages could not be resolved, downloaded, or verified, or a
bundle failed its offline install check.
Resolution failures are reported before anything is downloaded.
Packages that did download successfully are left in the output directory, so
a re-run resumes rather than starting over; consult standard error for a
per-package diagnosis.
.\"
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview what would be downloaded without writing any files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
packmule \-n \-f requirements.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Download all transitive dependencies for a Linux x86_64 target:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
packmule \-f requirements.txt \-o ./vendor \-a x86_64
.EE
.RE
.PP
Download and produce a self-contained transport bundle (vendor.tar.gz):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
packmule \-f requirements.txt \-o ./vendor \-a x86_64 \-\-bundle
.EE
.RE
.PP
Download Node.js dependencies and bundle them for transport:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
packmule \-f package.json \-o ./vendor \-t npm \-\-bundle
.EE
.RE
.PP
Download RPM packages from a Fedora 40 repository:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
packmule \-f packages.txt \-o ./rpms \-t rpm \-a x86_64 \e
    \-u https://dl.fedoraproject.org/pub/fedora/linux/releases/40/Everything/x86_64/os
.EE
.RE
.PP
Download Python packages from a private Artifactory mirror:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
packmule \-f requirements.txt \-o ./vendor \e
    \-u https://artifactory.example.com/artifactory/api/pypi/pypi-virtual/pypi
.EE
.RE
.\"
.SH NOTES
.B packmule
requires outbound HTTPS connectivity to the upstream registry on the machine
where it is run.
The output directory (and the bundle produced by
.BR \-\-bundle )
requires no registry access on the target machine.
.PP
When standard output is a terminal,
.B packmule
draws a single-line progress bar for each file as it downloads, showing the
percentage complete and the current transfer speed.
The bar redraws in place and is replaced by a permanent status line marking
success (with the filename and on-disk size) or failure (with the reason).
When standard output is not a terminal (a pipe, file, or log), the progress bar
and colour are suppressed and only the permanent per-package status lines are
written, so captured logs remain readable.
The dry-run mode
.RB ( \-n )
prints the full resolved list without a progress bar.
.PP
Re-runs are idempotent: a file already present in the output directory with a
matching digest is not downloaded again \(em it is reported with a
.B (cached)
marker.
Missing, truncated, or stale files are re-downloaded and overwritten, so an
interrupted run can simply be repeated.
.PP
Package name deduplication for the
.B pypi
backend follows PEP\~503: the characters
.BR \- ,
.BR _ ,
and
.B .
are treated as equivalent, and comparisons are case-insensitive.
.PP
For the
.B pypi
backend, PEP\~440 version specifiers
.RB ( == ", " >= ", " < ", " ~= ", " != ", " ==x.* )
are honoured: each package resolves to the highest release satisfying the
intersection of all ranges requested by its dependents.
Extras-gated transitive dependencies
.RI ( "; extra == 'security'" )
are followed only when the parent was requested with that extra.
OS and Python-version environment markers
.RI ( "; python_version > '3.8'" )
are evaluated against the target platform
.RB ( \-a ", " \-s ", " \-p );
markers that cannot be fully evaluated keep the dependency rather than risk
dropping one.
.PP
For the
.B npm
backend, exact versions are queried directly; range specifiers
.RB ( ^ ", " ~ ", " >= ", " 1.x ", " "a || b" )
resolve to the highest published version satisfying the range, and
dist-tags resolve through the packument.
Packages published before approximately 2017 that lack a
.B dist.integrity
SHA\-512 field are refused rather than falling back to the weaker SHA\-1
.B dist.shasum
field.
.PP
For the
.B rpm
backend, transitive dependencies are followed by default: each
.I <rpm:requires>
capability is matched against the
.I <rpm:provides>
and file lists published in the repository metadata, and one provider is
bundled per capability.
See
.B \-\-rpm\-deps
to turn this off.
Version constraints on a capability are compared using RPM epoch-version-release
ordering.
When an architecture-specific package and a
.B noarch
package are both available,
.B packmule
prefers the architecture-specific one.
.PP
When an architecture-specific package and a universal or source package are
both available for the
.B pypi
backend,
.B packmule
selects them in the following order: architecture-specific first, universal
second, source archive third.
This preference can be overridden with
.BR "\-a any" .
.\"
.SH FILES
.TP
.I manifest
Input file passed to
.BR \-f .
The format is determined by the active backend
.RB ( \-t ).
.TP
.I output_dir/
Destination for all downloaded package files.
.TP
.I output_dir/manifest.json
Machine-readable JSON index of every downloaded package, including resolved
version, filename, and SHA\-256 digest.
Written when
.B \-\-bundle
is given.
.TP
.I output_dir/SHA256SUMS
SHA\-256 digests of every package file and of the generated metadata, in
.BR sha256sum (1)
format.
Written when
.B \-\-bundle
is given, and checked by
.I install.sh
and by
.BR "packmule verify" .
.TP
.I output_dir/package-lock.json
Copy of the project's lockfile, consumed by
.I install.sh
via
.BR "npm ci \-\-offline" .
Written when
.B \-\-bundle
is given, the active backend is
.BR npm ,
and the manifest directory contained a lockfile.
.TP
.I output_dir/install.sh
Shell script for installing the bundle on the target machine.
Written when
.B \-\-bundle
is given; made executable (mode 0755).
.TP
.I output_dir/requirements.txt
pip-compatible requirements file consumed by
.IR install.sh .
Written when
.B \-\-bundle
is given and the active backend is
.BR pypi .
.TP
.IB output_dir .tar.gz
Compressed archive of the output directory, created when
.B \-\-bundle
is given.
.\"
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR openssl (1),
.BR pip (1),
.BR npm (1),
.BR rpm (8),
.BR dnf (8)
.\"
